WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.915
Task statement 5.3.

2
00:00:01.915 --> 00:00:06.829
Implement error propagation strategies across multi-agent systems.

3
00:00:06.829 --> 00:00:15.839
In a multi-agent research run, one subagent's silent failure can turn into a confident report with a hole nobody can see.

4
00:00:17.239 --> 00:00:20.033
In a multi-agent system, failures are normal.

5
00:00:20.033 --> 00:00:26.554
What matters is whether the information about a failure reaches the agent that can do something about it.

6
00:00:26.554 --> 00:00:32.018
If a subagent hides the error, the coordinator writes a confident but incomplete answer.

7
00:00:32.018 --> 00:00:34.937
If it crashes the whole run, good work is lost.

8
00:00:34.937 --> 00:00:38.849
The middle path is to report the failure clearly, with context.

9
00:00:40.249 --> 00:00:43.134
A useful failure report answers four questions.

10
00:00:43.134 --> 00:00:44.852
What kind of failure was it?

11
00:00:44.852 --> 00:00:45.895
What did you try?

12
00:00:45.895 --> 00:00:47.982
What did you get before it failed?

13
00:00:47.982 --> 00:00:50.620
And what could the coordinator try instead?

14
00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:54.793
Transient problems, like a timeout, should be retried locally first.

15
00:00:54.793 --> 00:00:59.641
Only when that fails does the subagent send a structured partial result upward.

16
00:01:01.041 --> 00:01:04.173
Two outcomes look similar but mean opposite things.

17
00:01:04.173 --> 00:01:07.735
A valid empty result says we checked and there is nothing.

18
00:01:07.735 --> 00:01:10.314
An access failure says we could not check.

19
00:01:10.314 --> 00:01:12.893
Mixing them up produces false conclusions.

20
00:01:12.893 --> 00:01:20.263
And when a gap cannot be filled, the final report should say so plainly, so readers know what the findings do not cover.

21
00:01:21.663 --> 00:01:26.184
In the research scenario, a subagent cannot open two of its six sources.

22
00:01:26.184 --> 00:01:34.599
It retries once, then returns the four sources it did read, marked as partial, with the access failure and two suggested alternatives.

23
00:01:34.599 --> 00:01:38.493
The coordinator sends another subagent after the alternatives.

24
00:01:38.493 --> 00:01:43.956
If those fail too, the final report names the gap instead of pretending it is complete.

25
00:01:45.356 --> 00:01:49.158
The exam frames this as two bad extremes and one good middle.

26
00:01:49.158 --> 00:01:52.711
Swallowing errors and crashing everything are both wrong.

27
00:01:52.711 --> 00:01:55.454
A bare failure message is not enough either.

28
00:01:55.454 --> 00:02:02.124
The right answer carries structured context upward and keeps the final output honest about what is missing.

29
00:02:03.524 --> 00:02:04.093
To recap.

30
00:02:04.093 --> 00:02:07.189
Failures should travel upward as structured data.

31
00:02:07.189 --> 00:02:10.791
Keep access failures distinct from genuine empty results.

32
00:02:10.791 --> 00:02:17.363
Retry transient problems close to the source, and pass everything else up with partial results attached.

33
00:02:17.363 --> 00:02:21.913
Finally, make the end product honest by listing what it could not cover.

